      <SubstanceNav id="ibogaine" />

      <main class="article-body">

        <!-- What Ibogaine Is -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="ibo-what">What Ibogaine Is</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Ibogaine is an indole alkaloid found primarily in the root bark of <em>Tabernanthe iboga</em>, a shrub native to the rainforests of Central and West Africa &mdash; principally Gabon, Cameroon, and the Republic of Congo. It also occurs in smaller concentrations in several related Apocynaceae species, including <em>Voacanga africana</em> and <em>Tabernaemontana undulata</em>. The compound was first isolated by French and Belgian chemists in 1901 and investigated sporadically through the early twentieth century before interest in its anti-addictive properties emerged in the 1960s.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Ibogaine is structurally classified as a <span class="tt" data-tip="A class of compounds sharing the core indole-ethylamine structure. Classical serotonergic psychedelics — psilocin, DMT, LSD, ibogaine — are tryptamines. The tryptamine scaffold is also the backbone of serotonin and melatonin.">tryptamine</span> but pharmacologically unique: its mechanism of action spans multiple receptor systems simultaneously, differentiating it fundamentally from <span class="tt" data-tip="Relating to the neurotransmitter serotonin and its receptors. Classical psychedelics — psilocybin, LSD, DMT, mescaline — are serotonergic: they produce their primary effects by activating serotonin receptors, particularly 5-HT₂A.">serotonergic</span> psychedelics like psilocybin and LSD. It is both psychoactive and, at relevant doses, a genuine medical intervention requiring clinical-level screening and monitoring. The distinction between ibogaine as a sacramental practice and ibogaine as a pharmacological treatment for addiction is real but the safety considerations apply in both contexts: the same compound, at comparable doses, produces the same cardiac risks regardless of setting.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Where It Comes From -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="ibo-sources">Where Ibogaine Comes From</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Ibogaine comes from the root bark of <em>Tabernanthe iboga</em>, a slow-growing understorey shrub of the Central African rainforest, concentrated in Gabon and reaching into Cameroon and the Republic of Congo. The alkaloids sit overwhelmingly in the root, and harvesting the root is what damages or kills the plant, which is the origin of most of the supply pressure described below.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Three forms that are not interchangeable</h3>
          <p class="content-p">This is the most important practical distinction on the page after the cardiac risk, and confusing these has killed people.</p>
          <ul class="content-list">
            <li><strong>Root bark</strong> is the traditional Bwiti preparation: shredded or powdered bark, taken by weight. Ibogaine content varies considerably with the plant, its age, and where on the root the bark came from, so bark cannot be dosed precisely by weight.</li>
            <li><strong>Total alkaloid extract</strong>, usually written TA, is a concentrated extract containing ibogaine alongside ibogaline, ibogamine, and other iboga alkaloids. It is far stronger than bark by weight and its composition varies by producer.</li>
            <li><strong>Ibogaine hydrochloride</strong>, written HCl, is the purified single compound. It is the form used in clinics because it can be dosed precisely, and it is stronger again by weight than TA.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="content-p">The consequence is arithmetic and unforgiving: a dose of bark, the same weight of TA, and the same weight of HCl differ several-fold in delivered ibogaine. Someone applying a bark dose to HCl has taken a massive overdose of a compound with real cardiac toxicity. The <a href="#ibo-testing" class="inline-link">verification section</a> covers how to establish which one you actually have.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">The <em>Voacanga</em> route</h3>
          <p class="content-p">A growing share of the world's ibogaine is not made from iboga at all. <em>Voacanga africana</em>, a related and far more common tree, yields voacangine, which can be converted to ibogaine semi-synthetically. Voacanga is faster growing, more widely distributed across West Africa, and harvested from bark and seed rather than root, so it does not carry iboga's conservation problem. Most clinical-grade ibogaine HCl now comes from this route.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Two cautions attach to it. <em>Voacanga</em>-derived total alkaloid preparations are sold in some jurisdictions as legal precursor material, and their alkaloid profile differs from <em>T. iboga</em> TA, so they are not a substitute for it at equivalent weight. And a semi-synthetic origin says nothing about purity: the conversion is a chemical process whose quality depends entirely on who performed it.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Supply pressure and the Gabonese position</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Wild iboga populations face habitat loss and harvesting pressure at the same time, and international demand from addiction treatment and psychedelic tourism has intensified both. Gabon has recognised iboga as a national cultural and heritage resource and restricted the export of raw material in response. Anyone sourcing iboga bark from Gabon outside that framework is participating in a trade the source country has explicitly moved to limit, which is a reason the <em>Voacanga</em>-derived route is the more defensible option on ethical grounds as well as ecological ones. The <a href="#ibo-ethics" class="inline-link">ethics section</a> takes this further.</p>
          <NatureCard id="ibogaine" />
        </div>

        <!-- History & Cultural Roots -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="ibo-history">History &amp; Cultural Roots</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Iboga has been at the centre of Bwiti &mdash; a complex spiritual and initiation tradition practiced principally by the Mitsogho, Fang, and related peoples of Gabon and southern Cameroon &mdash; for centuries, and possibly far longer. Iboga was documented by French and Belgian observers in the nineteenth century and the alkaloid was first isolated in 1901, but the tradition it came from long predates that encounter and has continued through it. The structure of Bwiti practice is described under <a href="#ibo-ceremony" class="inline-link">ceremony and container</a>.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Western discovery of ibogaine's anti-addictive properties is attributed to Howard Lotsof, a heroin-dependent man who in 1962 consumed ibogaine recreationally and found that his opioid craving had disappeared. Lotsof spent decades advocating for clinical research and developing treatment protocols, receiving several patents for ibogaine's use in addiction treatment. The US government classified ibogaine as a <span class="tt" data-tip="The most restrictive classification under the US Controlled Substances Act, indicating a substance with &quot;no accepted medical use and a high potential for abuse.&quot; Psilocybin, LSD, DMT, MDMA, and mescaline remain Schedule I in the US, significantly constraining research.">Schedule I</span> substance in 1970 as part of the Controlled Substances Act, effectively preventing clinical research there &mdash; most of the subsequent development of ibogaine treatment has occurred in Europe, Canada, Mexico, and more recently New Zealand.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Ceremony & Container -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="ibo-ceremony">Ceremony and Container</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Bwiti initiation is the oldest container for iboga and remains the most demanding use of any substance covered on this site. Very large quantities of root bark are given over the course of a night and into the following days, producing an experience that runs far longer than anything else here. It is framed as a rite of passage: a crossing into relationship with the ancestors, a confrontation with death, and an entry into adult spiritual life.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The container is not decoration around the dose. Music, song, and drumming continue through the night, a fire is kept, the community witnesses collectively, and trained initiates known as ngangas remain with the initiate throughout. Initiation is undertaken once, for a reason, at a point in someone's life, rather than repeated. Much of what a Western reader would call preparation and integration is built into the surrounding structure rather than added to it.</p>
          <div class="content-callout content-callout--warning">
            <div class="callout-label">Ceremonial framing does not reduce the cardiac risk</div>
            <p>This is the point on which the two contexts cannot be reconciled. Traditional practice developed without electrocardiograms, and iboga has caused deaths in ceremonial settings as well as clinical ones. The screening described under <a href="#ibo-risks" class="inline-link">risks and cardiac safety</a> applies to a Bwiti initiation exactly as it applies to a clinic, and a ceremony that does not ask about your heart is not safer for being traditional. Anyone considering initiation should arrange their own cardiac workup beforehand regardless of what the provider requires.</p>
          </div>
          <p class="content-p">A practical note for readers weighing this against a clinic: initiation and addiction treatment are different undertakings with different purposes, and Bwiti practitioners have not generally presented initiation as a detox procedure. Ceremonies marketed to outsiders as both at once are usually being shaped by the market rather than the tradition.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Ethics and Sovereignty -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="ibo-ethics">Ethics and Sovereignty</h2>
          <p class="content-p">Bwiti is a living, sovereign tradition rather than a relic. Custodianship of iboga knowledge, of the land it grows on, and of the plant itself are matters of cultural rights and political self-determination for the peoples who hold them. The commercialisation of iboga through Western addiction treatment and psychedelic tourism has raised serious objections from Bwiti knowledge holders about <span class="tt" data-tip="The appropriation of Indigenous knowledge — including plant medicine traditions, ceremonial practices, and cosmological frameworks — without acknowledgment, attribution, consent, or benefit-sharing with the originating communities.">epistemic extraction</span>, at the same time as outside demand has intensified pressure on wild populations already reduced by habitat loss.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Gabon has responded by recognising iboga as a national cultural and heritage resource and restricting the export of raw material. That is a sovereign decision by the country the plant and the tradition come from, and it is worth treating as such rather than as an obstacle to be routed around.</p>
          <p class="content-p">What this resolves into for a reader is more concrete than it is for most substances on this site, because there is a genuine choice available at the point of sourcing. Ibogaine derived from <a href="#ibo-sources" class="inline-link"><em>Voacanga africana</em></a> does not draw on wild iboga root, does not depend on material Gabon has restricted, and is what most clinical-grade supply already uses. Choosing it costs the reader nothing and removes them from the part of the trade that is doing the damage.</p>
          <p class="content-p">If you are considering initiation rather than treatment, the questions are the ones that apply to any living tradition: who is the practitioner accountable to, does the community they claim recognise them, and does any of what you pay return to the people whose tradition this is. A ceremony run for outsiders by someone with no accountability to a Bwiti community is selling the aesthetics of a container without the thing that makes a container work.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- How Ibogaine Works -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="ibo-how">How Ibogaine Works</h2>
          <h3 class="content-h3">Multi-receptor pharmacology</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Ibogaine's pharmacology is extraordinarily complex and not fully characterised. Its known mechanisms include: antagonism at <span class="tt" data-tip="N-methyl-D-aspartate receptor — a glutamate receptor involved in synaptic plasticity and memory formation. Ketamine produces its dissociative and antidepressant effects primarily by blocking NMDA receptors, a fundamentally different mechanism from classical psychedelics.">NMDA</span> glutamate receptors (which may contribute to its <span class="tt" data-tip="A class of psychoactive substances — including ketamine, PCP, and nitrous oxide — that produce detachment from sensory experience and a sense of separation from the body or environment. Distinct from classical serotonergic psychedelics in mechanism and phenomenological character.">dissociative</span> and oneirogenic qualities); agonism at kappa-opioid receptors (contributing to intense imagery and, in some models, to its anti-addictive effects); modulation of sigma receptors; antagonism at nicotinic acetylcholine receptors; and serotonin reuptake inhibition. It also affects sodium channels in the heart &mdash; the mechanism underlying its most serious risk &mdash; and is metabolised hepatically to noribogaine, an active metabolite with a much longer half-life (days to weeks) that continues exerting effects after ibogaine itself has cleared.</p>
          <p class="content-p">Unlike serotonergic psychedelics, ibogaine is not primarily a 5-HT2A <span class="tt" data-tip="A compound that binds to and activates a receptor, mimicking the effect of its natural signalling molecule. Classical psychedelics are partial agonists at the 5-HT₂A serotonin receptor.">agonist</span> and produces phenomenological effects quite unlike those of psilocybin or LSD. Its kappa-opioid and NMDA-antagonist actions are thought to be more central to its unusual experiential profile &mdash; the waking dream state, the life review quality, and the atypical visual phenomenology.</p>
          <h3 class="content-h3">The interruption of opioid dependence</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Ibogaine's most clinically significant property is its capacity to dramatically reduce or eliminate opioid withdrawal symptoms and interrupt physical opioid dependence following a single dose. The precise mechanism remains under active investigation. Noribogaine's long-acting effects at opioid receptors likely contribute. Current models propose that ibogaine resets opioid receptor sensitivity and modulates the neural circuits of habit and craving &mdash; particularly in the ventral tegmental area and nucleus accumbens &mdash; through a combination of NMDA antagonism, kappa-opioid activity, and glutamate system normalisation. The result, observed consistently across clinical and observational studies, is an interruption of withdrawal within hours and a reduction in craving that can persist for weeks to months following a single treatment session.</p>
          <h3 class="content-h3">18-MC and the non-psychedelic derivative</h3>
          <p class="content-p">18-Methoxycoronaridine (18-MC), a synthetic iboga alkaloid congener developed by Stanley Glick, retains anti-addictive properties without the psychedelic experience and, crucially, without the cardiac effects. It is in Phase II clinical trials. Its development is partly motivated by the hope of producing an FDA-approvable anti-addiction medicine without the safety and regulatory challenges of ibogaine itself &mdash; though some researchers and practitioners believe the psychedelic experience is itself therapeutically necessary, not a side effect to be engineered away.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Effects -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="ibo-effects">Effects</h2>
          <h3 class="content-h3">Three phases of the experience</h3>
          <p class="content-p">The ibogaine experience is characteristically divided into three overlapping phases. The <strong>acute phase</strong> (roughly hours 1&ndash;8) involves intense oneiric (waking dream) visual phenomena &mdash; vivid, autonomous imagery distinct from the geometric or emotional visuals of serotonergic psychedelics, often described as panoramic scenes, memories, or mythological narratives playing out with unusual clarity and apparent significance. These visuals occur with eyes open or closed and are accompanied by significant ataxia (loss of coordination) and often nausea or vomiting. The physical incapacitation during the acute phase is such that most people are bed-bound; attempting to walk without support is dangerous. Sound is typically amplified &mdash; sometimes painfully so &mdash; and music becomes intensely meaningful.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The <strong>evaluative phase</strong> (hours 8&ndash;20) tends to shift from autonomous visual phenomena toward introspection and what is widely described as a "life review" &mdash; a panoramic revisiting of significant memories, relationships, and patterns in one's history, often with a quality of emotional perspective or detachment not available in ordinary consciousness. Many people describe this as the therapeutically significant portion of the experience: a kind of inventory of one's life, with material surfacing that feels relevant to patterns of use, relational dynamics, or unresolved grief.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The <strong>residual stimulation phase</strong> can persist for 24&ndash;48 hours beyond the acute experience. People often find sleep impossible for the first night or two. There is a characteristic reflective openness during this window &mdash; energy, mental clarity, and emotional accessibility &mdash; that many providers regard as a primary integration opportunity. Total duration from ingestion to return to baseline sleep capacity: typically 24&ndash;36 hours, occasionally longer. This is substantially longer than any commonly used serotonergic psychedelic.</p>
          <h3 class="content-h3">Phenomenological character</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Ibogaine's phenomenological character is distinctive and should not be assimilated to the psilocybin or ayahuasca experience. The waking dream state is autonomous and image-driven in a way that many people describe as less controllable than serotonergic psychedelic experiences. The life review quality is widely reported but variable &mdash; some people experience it centrally, others less so. Physical symptoms (ataxia, nausea, sensitivity to light and sound) are more prominent than in most serotonergic psychedelic experiences. Confrontation with difficult emotional and biographical material is characteristic and should be anticipated rather than feared &mdash; preparation to receive and work with this material is part of responsible ibogaine therapy.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Dosage Reference -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="ibo-dosage">Dosage Reference</h2>
          <p class="content-p"><em>These ranges apply to pure ibogaine hydrochloride administered in clinical or medically supervised contexts. Root bark extracts are not reliably dose-calculable by weight. This table is reference information, not a recommendation.</em></p>
          <div class="table-scroll"><table class="dosage-table">
            <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Ibogaine HCl (by body weight)</th><th>Character</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Low / test dose</td><td>1&ndash;3 mg/kg</td><td>Mild psychoactive effects. Used to assess sensitivity and cardiac response before full dose.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Sub-flood (psychoactive)</td><td>5&ndash;8 mg/kg</td><td>Significant psychedelic effects without complete flood experience. Used in some protocols for psychological work.</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Flood dose (addiction interruption)</td><td>10&ndash;20 mg/kg</td><td>Full experience; typical clinical dose for opioid dependence interruption. Maximum cardiac risk occurs at this range.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p class="content-p">Flood doses are weight-adjusted and should only be administered in settings with cardiac monitoring equipment and personnel capable of managing arrhythmia. A test dose administered 24&ndash;48 hours before a full flood dose allows assessment of <span class="tt" data-tip="An abnormal lengthening of the QT interval on an ECG, indicating delayed ventricular repolarisation. Increases the risk of potentially fatal arrhythmias — the primary cardiac safety concern with ibogaine.">QTc</span> interval response. No responsible ibogaine provider should administer a flood dose without a baseline ECG, full contraindication screening, and continuous cardiac monitoring capability during the session.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- What the Research Shows -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="ibo-research">What the Research Shows</h2>
          <h3 class="content-h3">Opioid use disorder</h3>
          <p class="content-p">The evidence base for ibogaine's anti-addictive effects is substantial but consists primarily of observational studies, case series, and <span class="tt" data-tip="A trial design in which both researchers and participants know which treatment is being administered — the opposite of a blind trial. Open-label studies can inflate effect sizes due to expectancy; most early psychedelic trials were open-label, a key limitation.">open-label</span> trials rather than <span class="tt" data-tip="The gold standard of clinical evidence: participants are randomly assigned to receive either the active treatment or a control condition, allowing causal inferences about treatment effects while controlling for confounds.">randomised controlled trial</span>s &mdash; largely because Schedule I status and safety concerns have made RCTs extremely difficult to conduct. Nonetheless, the consistency of findings across studies, countries, and decades is striking. A landmark Stanford-affiliated study published in <em>Nature Medicine</em> (Cherian et al., 2024) examined outcomes in veterans treated with ibogaine at a clinic in Mexico and found large reductions in PTSD symptom severity, depression, and anxiety &mdash; an extraordinary effect size in a difficult-to-treat population. Alper and colleagues' review of global case reports and observational data found that approximately half of opioid-dependent individuals who underwent ibogaine treatment reported no withdrawal symptoms and significant reductions in craving, with some maintaining abstinence at follow-up periods of months to years. Mash and colleagues' observational data from Bahamian and Caribbean treatment centres found comparable patterns across hundreds of patients.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The durability of ibogaine's anti-addictive effects appears highly variable and dependent on post-treatment support. People who receive ibogaine without accompanying psychological support, integration therapy, or ongoing community resources tend to have higher relapse rates than those who enter structured aftercare. Ibogaine interrupts the physical dependence and acute craving &mdash; it does not resolve the social, psychological, and environmental drivers of addiction that will continue to operate in the post-treatment period.</p>
          <h3 class="content-h3">PTSD, depression, and TBI</h3>
          <p class="content-p">The Stanford <em>Nature Medicine</em> study (2024) examined a cohort of special operations veterans treated at a legally operating ibogaine clinic in Mexico, finding dramatic reductions not only in PTSD symptoms but in suicidality, functional disability, and cognitive performance on neuropsychological testing. These findings generated significant attention given the <span class="tt" data-tip="Major depressive disorder that has not responded adequately to at least two different antidepressant treatments. The population for which psilocybin and ketamine clinical evidence is strongest.">treatment-resistant</span> profile of the population and the scale of effect sizes reported. Controlled replication is urgently needed. Several institutions are currently developing ibogaine research programmes, including <span class="tt" data-tip="Multidisciplinary Association for Psychedelic Studies — a US nonprofit that has funded and coordinated the largest clinical trials for MDMA-assisted therapy for PTSD, and advocates for psychedelic science and policy reform.">MAPS</span> and academic centres in Canada, Switzerland, and New Zealand.</p>
          <h3 class="content-h3">Microdosing iboga</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Sub-perceptual doses of iboga root bark, used within Bwiti practice for maintenance and community ceremonies rather than initiation, have attracted interest from harm reduction and addiction communities. Observational data suggest that low-dose iboga may help maintain abstinence after a flood-dose treatment. This is a poorly studied area and not a substitute for medical management of addiction or psychiatric conditions.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Risks & Cardiac Safety -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="ibo-risks">Risks &amp; Cardiac Safety</h2>
          <div class="content-callout content-callout--warning">
            <div class="callout-label">Critical safety information</div>
            <p>Ibogaine prolongs the QTc interval of the cardiac cycle &mdash; the period during which the heart's electrical system resets between beats. <span class="tt" data-tip="An abnormal lengthening of the QT interval on an ECG, indicating delayed ventricular repolarisation. Increases the risk of potentially fatal arrhythmias — the primary cardiac safety concern with ibogaine.">QTc prolongation</span> increases the risk of ventricular arrhythmias, including Torsades de Pointes and ventricular fibrillation, which can cause sudden cardiac death. This is not a theoretical risk: ibogaine-associated deaths have occurred and are documented in the literature. Estimates of treatment-associated mortality vary but the most cited figure is approximately 1 in 300 treatments in contexts without adequate medical screening &mdash; a rate that is substantially reduced, but not eliminated, by proper cardiac screening and monitoring. <strong>There is no safe unsupervised ibogaine use at flood doses.</strong></p>
          </div>
          <h3 class="content-h3">Absolute cardiac contraindications</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Any pre-existing prolonged QTc interval (above 450ms in men, 470ms in women) is an absolute contraindication. Structural heart disease, arrhythmias, heart failure, and a family or personal history of sudden cardiac death also represent absolute contraindications. Before any flood-dose ibogaine administration, a 12-lead ECG is mandatory. Electrolyte imbalances &mdash; particularly low potassium or magnesium, which are common in people with substance use disorders &mdash; must be corrected before treatment, as electrolyte abnormalities independently increase arrhythmia risk and potentiate ibogaine's cardiac effects.</p>
          <h3 class="content-h3">Drug interactions</h3>
          <div class="content-callout content-callout--warning">
            <div class="interaction-callout-icon">&#9888;&#65039;</div>
            <div class="interaction-callout-body">
              <strong>Ibogaine has life-threatening drug interactions</strong>
              <p>QTc-prolonging medications &mdash; including methadone, SSRIs, antipsychotics, and others &mdash; can be fatal in combination with ibogaine. Review all medications with a physician before any ibogaine treatment.</p>
              <a href="/assessment/#ass-combochart" class="ic-link">View full drug interaction chart &rarr;</a>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="content-p">Methadone, in particular, significantly prolongs QTc and the combination with ibogaine represents a substantial cardiac risk. Methadone-maintained individuals typically require a medically supervised taper to shorter-acting opioids (most often buprenorphine) before ibogaine treatment &mdash; a process that itself requires medical management. SSRIs, TCAs, antipsychotics, and many other medications also prolong QTc and require careful assessment. Stimulants &mdash; particularly cocaine and methamphetamine &mdash; present cardiovascular risks in the ibogaine context. The combination with <span class="tt" data-tip="Monoamine oxidase inhibitors — compounds that block the breakdown of serotonin, dopamine, and other neurotransmitters. Critical safety consideration with ayahuasca; potentially fatal in combination with SSRIs and many common medications.">MAOIs</span> (present in ayahuasca) is dangerous and should be avoided.</p>
          <h3 class="content-h3">Psychological risks</h3>
          <p class="content-p">The ibogaine experience is intense and often psychologically demanding. People with active <span class="tt" data-tip="A mental state characterised by a loss of contact with shared reality — involving hallucinations, delusions, and disorganised thinking. A personal or family history of psychosis or schizophrenia spectrum disorders is a primary contraindication for psychedelic use.">psychosis</span> or schizophrenia spectrum conditions should not receive ibogaine. Severe, untreated mood disorders warrant careful assessment. The life review nature of the experience means that traumatic material frequently surfaces &mdash; integration support before and after is not optional for responsible practice.</p>
          <h3 class="content-h3">Iboga plant sustainability</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Wild <em>Tabernanthe iboga</em> populations are under pressure from overharvesting driven by international demand. The slow growth rate of the plant (root bark of therapeutic potency requires 7&ndash;10+ years of growth) and habitat loss in Central Africa have created a genuine sustainability crisis. Responsible engagement with ibogaine means giving weight to sourcing: providers and individuals who use cultivated or sustainably sourced iboga, or who work with <em>Voacanga africana</em>-derived total alkaloid preparations or synthetic ibogaine where clinically equivalent, contribute less to this pressure.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Verifying What You Have -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="ibo-testing">Verifying What You Have</h2>
          <p class="content-p">For most substances on this site, verification means answering "is this the compound it is supposed to be." For ibogaine that question matters, but a second one matters more: <strong>which preparation is this, and how much ibogaine is actually in it</strong>. The dosage table above is expressed in milligrams per kilogram of body weight of <em>pure ibogaine hydrochloride</em>. Applying those numbers to a different preparation produces an error of several fold, and because the dangerous effects are cardiac and dose-dependent, an error in that direction is not recoverable by waiting it out.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">The three forms are not interchangeable by weight</h3>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>Ibogaine hydrochloride (HCl)</strong> is the purified salt, and is what clinical protocols and the mg/kg figures above assume. <strong>Total alkaloid (TA) extract</strong> contains ibogaine alongside ibogaline, ibogamine, tabernanthine and others, and ibogaine is only a portion of the total, and that portion varies substantially between producers and batches. <strong>Powdered root bark</strong> contains ibogaine at a small percentage of its dry weight, varying with the age of the plant, which part of the root it came from, and where it was grown.</p>
          <p class="content-p">A gram of root bark, a gram of TA, and a gram of HCl therefore deliver dramatically different amounts of the active compound. "One gram of ibogaine" is not a meaningful statement unless the form is specified. If a supplier will not tell you which of the three they are selling, that is the end of the conversation.</p>
          <p class="content-p">There is a further wrinkle worth knowing: most ibogaine in circulation is semi-synthesised from <em>Voacanga africana</em> rather than extracted from <em>Tabernanthe iboga</em>. As pure HCl this makes no difference, since ibogaine is ibogaine regardless of which plant the precursor came from. But <em>Voacanga</em>-derived total alkaloid preparations have a different alkaloid profile from <em>T. iboga</em> TA, so "TA" does not describe a single, consistent product either.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Reagents will not help you here</h3>
          <p class="content-p">This is worth saying plainly, because reagent testing is the default assumption people bring from other substances. There is no established colour-reagent protocol that reliably identifies ibogaine, distinguishes it from the other iboga alkaloids, or tells you anything about concentration. The tools that work for MDMA or LSD do not transfer to this problem.</p>

          <div class="content-callout content-callout--warning">
            <div class="callout-label">Ask for a batch-matched certificate of analysis</div>
            <p>Meaningful verification of ibogaine means laboratory quantification by HPLC or GC/MS, reported on a <strong>certificate of analysis (CoA)</strong> from an independent lab. A legitimate clinic or supplier will have one and will provide it without resistance.</p>
            <p>Check three things on it: that it states <strong>ibogaine content as a percentage by weight</strong>, that it names <strong>the form</strong> (HCl, TA, or bark), and above all that its <strong>batch or lot number matches the material you are actually being given</strong>. A CoA for a different lot tells you about someone else's material, not yours. A generic, undated certificate reused across batches is a meaningful warning sign about the operation as a whole.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Verifying the setting is part of verifying the substance</h3>
          <p class="content-p">With ibogaine, a correct and well-characterised batch does not make an experience safe on its own. The same dose that is manageable with cardiac monitoring, corrected electrolytes and a physician present is the dose that kills people without them. The screening and monitoring requirements are set out in full under <a href="#ibo-risks" class="inline-link">Risks &amp; Cardiac Safety</a> above, and the questions to put to a provider are covered under <a href="#ibo-access" class="inline-link">Accessing Ibogaine Treatment Safely</a> below. Treat a provider who can produce a certificate of analysis but cannot produce an ECG and a baseline electrolyte panel as having answered the easier half of the question.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Legal Status -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="ibo-legal">Legal Status</h2>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>United States:</strong> Schedule I controlled substance. No legal therapeutic use. Research possible only with DEA Schedule I researcher licence &mdash; a significant barrier. Underground treatment exists but carries both legal and safety risk.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>Canada:</strong> Not currently scheduled under the <span class="tt" data-tip="The Controlled Drugs and Substances Act — Canada's primary federal legislation governing controlled substances, equivalent in function to the US Controlled Substances Act. Psilocybin, psilocin, DMT, MDMA, and mescaline are all Schedule III substances under the CDSA.">Controlled Drugs and Substances Act</span>, placing it in a regulatory grey area. Clinical research and some treatment contexts operate with relative legal freedom, though the regulatory landscape is evolving.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>New Zealand:</strong> Reclassified as a Class C substance in 2009, permitting use in authorised treatment settings. The most permissive regulatory environment for ibogaine treatment among English-speaking countries.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>Mexico:</strong> Not scheduled. Numerous ibogaine treatment centres operate legally, making Mexico the primary destination for Americans seeking ibogaine treatment. Quality and safety standards vary enormously between providers.</p>
          <p class="content-p"><strong>Netherlands, Portugal, Brazil, and several other countries:</strong> Not scheduled or in grey areas that permit clinical and retreat use. <strong>UK, most of Europe, and Australia:</strong> Controlled substance; illegal without specific licence.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- Accessing Treatment -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="ibo-access">Accessing Ibogaine Treatment Safely</h2>
          <p class="content-p">For individuals considering ibogaine for opioid dependence or other indications, the most important decision is the choice of provider. This is a context where quality of screening and medical oversight is literally a matter of survival.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The GITA (Global Ibogaine Therapy Alliance) has developed safety guidelines and a provider directory. MAPS and related harm reduction organisations have published screening protocols. Minimum safety standards include: mandatory pre-treatment ECG; measurement and correction of electrolyte levels; comprehensive medication review and washout; continuous cardiac monitoring during the session; appropriate emergency equipment (defibrillator) and trained personnel; a structured integration programme before and after treatment; and a licensed medical professional present or immediately available throughout. Any provider who cannot demonstrate these standards should not be trusted with flood-dose ibogaine administration.</p>
          <p class="content-p">The MAPS-sponsored <span class="tt" data-tip="The second phase of clinical drug trials, testing a treatment in a larger group of participants (typically dozens to hundreds) to assess efficacy and side effects. Most psychedelic clinical evidence — including early psilocybin depression trials — comes from Phase 2.">Phase 2</span> trial of ibogaine is currently recruiting at sites in the United States, and academic research programmes at NYU, Johns Hopkins, and several Canadian universities are active. Clinical trial participation, where accessible, offers the highest-safety research-grade screening and monitoring.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- If You're Considering This -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="ibo-considering">If You Are Considering This</h2>
          <p class="content-p">General groundwork is on <a href="/assessment/" class="inline-link">assessment</a>, <a href="/preparation/" class="inline-link">preparation</a>, and <a href="/integration/" class="inline-link">integration</a>. Ibogaine departs from everything else on this site in one respect that governs all the rest: it can stop your heart, and no amount of psychological preparation addresses that.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">The cardiac workup comes before every other decision</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Ibogaine prolongs the QT interval, and the deaths associated with it are overwhelmingly cardiac. Before choosing a provider, before booking anything, get an ECG and have your electrolytes measured, particularly potassium and magnesium. Bring those results with you. A pre-existing conduction abnormality, an electrolyte deficit, or a medication that also prolongs QT can turn a standard dose into a fatal one, and none of those announce themselves. The <a href="#ibo-risks" class="inline-link">risks section</a> sets out the specifics. This is the one substance here where arranging your own medical screening in advance is the baseline rather than a precaution.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Do not do this alone, and do not do it at home</h3>
          <p class="content-p">A flood dose produces roughly twenty-four to thirty-six hours of profound incapacity, ataxia, vomiting, and an inability to stand, with a cardiac risk window running through most of it. Continuous monitoring and someone able to respond to an arrhythmia are not optional extras. Solo or unsupervised ibogaine, at any meaningful dose, is the single most dangerous thing described anywhere on this site.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Know which of the three forms you have</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Root bark, TA extract, and HCl differ several-fold in strength by weight, as set out under <a href="#ibo-sources" class="inline-link">where it comes from</a>. Applying a dose figure from the wrong form is a straightforward route to an overdose of a cardiotoxic compound. A provider who cannot tell you which form they use, at what purity, and on what basis they arrived at your dose has failed the most basic question you can ask them.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">If the goal is opioid detox, the plan matters more than the dose</h3>
          <p class="content-p">Ibogaine's reputation rests on interrupting withdrawal and craving, and for many people it does. What it does not do is keep working on its own. Tolerance drops sharply afterwards, which means a return to a previous opioid dose carries a serious overdose risk, and this is a well-documented cause of death in the weeks following treatment. Anyone going into ibogaine for opioid dependence needs the aftercare arranged before they go in: where they are staying, who is with them, what support is in place, and what happens at week four. A clinic without an answer to that is selling the easy part.</p>

          <h3 class="content-h3">Choose the source deliberately</h3>
          <p class="content-p">The <a href="#ibo-ethics" class="inline-link">ethics section</a> explains why <em>Voacanga</em>-derived ibogaine is the more defensible option, and it costs you nothing to ask a provider where theirs comes from. It is also a reasonable proxy for how carefully they run everything else.</p>

          <p class="content-p">Get the ECG, get the electrolytes, know the form, and have the month afterwards planned. Ibogaine is the one substance here where the preparation is mostly medical, and where skipping it has killed people who were otherwise well prepared.</p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div class="content-section">
          <h2 class="content-h2" id="ibo-faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="faq-section">

            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-question">
                <span>Can ibogaine cure opioid addiction?</span>
                <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
              </div>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>Ibogaine is not a cure for opioid addiction &mdash; that framing misrepresents what the research shows and sets people up for disappointment. What ibogaine can do, reliably and distinctively, is interrupt physical opioid dependence and dramatically reduce acute withdrawal symptoms following a single treatment. Many people report significantly reduced or absent craving for weeks to months afterward. However, addiction is not solely physical dependence: its psychological, social, and environmental drivers remain after ibogaine, and relapse rates without aftercare support are high. The most responsible framing is that ibogaine is a powerful pharmacological interruption that creates a window of opportunity &mdash; what is done in that window determines long-term outcomes.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-question">
                <span>How does ibogaine compare to other addiction treatments like methadone or buprenorphine?</span>
                <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
              </div>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>Methadone and buprenorphine are evidence-based opioid agonist therapies &mdash; they reduce craving and withdrawal by substituting a longer-acting, medically managed opioid. They are highly effective at reducing overdose mortality and improving quality of life, and represent the gold standard of opioid use disorder treatment. Ibogaine is pharmacologically opposite in its approach: a single high-dose treatment that interrupts physical dependence rather than managing it on an ongoing basis. These are different interventions with different profiles of evidence, risk, and appropriate use. For some people, ibogaine offers a path that agonist therapy alone has not &mdash; but these approaches can also be sequenced and combined (with careful attention to the methadone-to-buprenorphine-to-ibogaine transition process when needed). The framing of ibogaine as an alternative to established treatments risks leading people away from life-saving evidence-based care; it is better understood as a complementary option with its own serious risks and specific indications.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-question">
                <span>Is ibogaine dangerous? How many people have died from it?</span>
                <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
              </div>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>Yes, ibogaine carries a genuine risk of death &mdash; primarily from cardiac arrhythmia (ventricular fibrillation or Torsades de Pointes), which is caused by its QTc-prolonging effect. The most frequently cited estimate of treatment-associated mortality in the pre-screening era is approximately 1 in 300 administrations; with proper cardiac screening, electrolyte correction, and monitoring, this figure is substantially reduced. Tracking exact mortality rates is difficult because most ibogaine treatment occurs outside registered clinical trials, in countries with variable reporting infrastructure. The documented cases share common factors: inadequate pre-treatment screening, unmanaged drug interactions (particularly with methadone), electrolyte imbalances, and absence of medical monitoring during the experience. These risks are largely, though not entirely, preventable with proper protocols.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-question">
                <span>Is ibogaine a psychedelic?</span>
                <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
              </div>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>Ibogaine is psychoactive and produces a profoundly altered state of consciousness at therapeutic doses, but its pharmacological mechanism differs fundamentally from serotonergic psychedelics. It is sometimes categorised as an "atypical psychedelic" or an "oneirogen" (dream-inducer). Its primary actions are at NMDA, kappa-opioid, and sigma receptors, not at 5-HT2A receptors. The phenomenological experience &mdash; waking dream imagery, life review, ataxia, intense sound sensitivity &mdash; is distinctive from psilocybin, LSD, or ayahuasca and should not be compared to them in terms of character, preparation, or outcome expectations.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-question">
                <span>What should I do if I'm considering ibogaine for opioid addiction?</span>
                <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
              </div>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>First: consult with an addiction medicine physician before discontinuing any current medication, including methadone or buprenorphine. Abrupt discontinuation without medical supervision is dangerous. Second: research providers rigorously &mdash; use the GITA provider directory, verify that any clinic you consider offers mandatory pre-treatment ECG, electrolyte testing, continuous cardiac monitoring, and has medical personnel on-site. Third: ask about the integration programme &mdash; what support exists before and after the treatment itself? Providers without structured aftercare should be treated with significant scepticism. Fourth: consider clinical trial participation if you are in a jurisdiction where trials are recruiting &mdash; this offers research-grade safety protocols and typically at no cost. The MAPS Phase 2 ibogaine trial and the University of British Columbia's ibogaine programme (for veterans) are among those actively recruiting.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <div class="faq-item">
              <div class="faq-question">
                <span>What is the Bwiti tradition and how should Westerners relate to it?</span>
                <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
              </div>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>Bwiti is a living Indigenous spiritual tradition, not a historical practice. Its custodians &mdash; the Mitsogho, Fang, and related peoples of Gabon and Cameroon &mdash; retain sovereign authority over their knowledge, practice, and the iboga plant itself. Westerners engaging with ibogaine for addiction treatment or psychological healing are not participating in Bwiti &mdash; they are receiving a pharmacological treatment derived from a plant sacred to that tradition. Treating ibogaine with respect means acknowledging this: not appropriating Bwiti cultural forms, not claiming participation in a tradition one has not been initiated into, supporting Bwiti knowledge holders' rights and advocacy regarding iboga <span class="tt" data-tip="In the context of Indigenous peoples and psychedelics, sovereignty refers to the right of Indigenous nations to govern their own territories, knowledge systems, ceremonial practices, and relationships with plant medicines — without interference or appropriation by outside institutions.">sovereignty</span>, and where possible, contributing to sustainable iboga cultivation rather than fuelling pressure on wild populations. The Bwiti have explicitly called for recognition of their custodianship within international drug policy discussions; their voice belongs in those conversations.</p>
              </div>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

